The crash is: `sdb_free` → `ns_free` → `sdb_ns_free`

This means the crash happens during `sdb_free` which calls `sdb_fini` which calls `sdb_ns_free` which calls `ns_free`. Inside `ns_free`, there's a use-after-free.

Let me look at the `sdb_free` function again:

```c
SDB_API bool sdb_free(Sdb* s) {
    if (s && s->ht && s->refs) {
        s->refs--;
        if (s->refs < 1) {
            s->refs = 0;
            sdb_fini (s, 0);
            s->ht = NULL;
            free (s);
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

And `sdb_fini`:
```c
static void sdb_fini(Sdb* s, int donull) {
    ...
    sdb_ns_free (s);
    s->refs = 0;
    free (s->name);
    free (s->path);
    ls_free (s->ns);
    ...
}
```

And `sdb_ns_free`:
```c
SDB_API void sdb_ns_free(Sdb *s) {
    SdbList *list;
    if (!s) return;
    list = ls_new ();
    list->free = NULL;
    ns_free (s, list);
    ls_free (list);
    ls_free (s->ns);
    s->ns = NULL;
}
```

So the call chain is: `sdb_free(s)` → `sdb_fini(s)` → `sdb_ns_free(s)` → `ns_free(s, list)`.

Inside `ns_free`, when processing a namespace entry, it calls `sdb_free(ns->sdb)` at line 50. If this returns true (refs reached 0), the Sdb is freed and `ns->sdb` is set to NULL. Then `ls_append(list, NULL)`, `ns_free(NULL, list)`, and `sdb_free(NULL)` are all safe.

But if `sdb_free(ns->sdb)` at line 50 returns false (refs > 0), then `ns->sdb` is NOT set to NULL. Then `ns_free(ns->sdb, list)` is called recursively. Inside that recursive call, the Sdb could be freed (if its refs drop to 0 through processing its own namespaces). Then after returning from the recursive call, `sdb_free(ns->sdb)` at line 59 is called on the potentially freed pointer.

